--- What each mutation shows ---
M1 the substring matcher back: the three decoys whose arguments carry the words are taken for ours — the
finding, reproduced — while the real processes still pass, which is why this needed its own check rather than
a stricter pattern. M2 the check refuses everything: its own engine and its own Caddy fail, which is the
counterweight — a check is not "safer" for refusing the processes it exists to recognise. M3 the caddy half of
the case is dropped: a real Caddy is refused.

--- The finding's example, measured ---
`vim Caddyfile` does **not** match the old matcher, because it was case-sensitive: the word has to be
lowercase, as in `vim caddyfile`, which the probe runs beside it. The class of defect the finding describes is
real and reproduces twice over (the two renamed shells), so the fix stands; what is corrected is the example,
not the finding.

